WIRE IT: production composition, then listener registration.

(1) `apps/daemon/src/daemon-repository-workflow-wiring.ts:21-22` — the port literal currently reads `{ boundProjectId, readCriteria, readRecovery }`. Add `readBootstrap: () => readBootstrapReceipt(options.store, options.projectId)`. THIS IS THE EDIT THAT MAKES THE OPTIONAL METHOD REAL; without it every unit arm can pass against a hand-built port while the route is dead in production (risk B).

(2) `apps/daemon/src/http/http-listener-read-dispatch.ts` — four coupled edits, and the roster arms red if you make fewer than all four:
  - import `REPOSITORY_BOOTSTRAP_READ_PATH` alongside the existing two from `./repository-workflow-read.js` (the import at :35);
  - add it to the `JSON_ROUTES` frozen array (:68-99);
  - add its non-POST guard beside the one at :508 — `UNGUARDED_MEMBERS` is a frozen census, not a subset check, so a rostered path with no guard reds the arm at test :332 and would otherwise accept a GET;
  - add its dispatch branch beside :575, reusing the existing `serveRepositoryWorkflow(response, request, options, body, "BOOTSTRAP")` helper and widening that helper's `workflow` parameter type.
The chain's terminal `else serveDocumentDossier(...)` MUST stay an unconditional else — the roster's own bidirectional consistency depends on it and the arm at test :315 reads this file as source text.

Run `pnpm typecheck` at the end of this step and quote its output. The `$ tsc` script echo is the execution evidence; a sub-second green with no echo is not proof it ran.

